Deliberately not an open-source licence, for the same reason as Farewatch and
Greenlight: this was built as a side project with the intention that it might
eventually earn money, and a permissive licence would let anyone stand up a
competing service from the same code. If that stops being the goal, MIT is a
one-file change.

This notice covers the code. It does not cover the data in data/, which is
derived from three public sources and remains subject to their terms:

  * V-Dem democracy indices, via Our World in Data.
  * Human Development Index indicators, from the United Nations Development
    Programme.
  * Environmental Performance Index categories and weights, from Yale.

Those tables are national-level published statistics rather than microdata, and
each is rebuildable from its source with the matching tools/fetch_*.py. They are
included so a clone can reproduce the results in the README without re-fetching.
Attribution for them belongs to the three publishers above, and nothing in this
notice grants rights over their data that Assay does not hold.

The tables are adapted (selected, reshaped and renamed) from each source, and
are licensed under that source's own terms, read 2026-09-27:

  * data/vdem-2024.*  - V-Dem Dataset, Varieties of Democracy Institute, via
    Our World in Data. CC BY-SA 4.0. These files are shared under CC BY-SA 4.0.
  * data/hdi-*        - Human Development Report data, UNDP Human Development
    Report Office. CC BY 3.0 IGO.
  * data/epi-*        - 2024 Environmental Performance Index, Yale Center for
    Environmental Law & Policy and Columbia CIESIN. CC BY-NC-SA 4.0: these files
    are shared under CC BY-NC-SA 4.0 and may not be used commercially. If Assay
    is ever sold or run for profit, the EPI tables and any result built on them
    must come out, or Yale's permission be obtained.

